  What's here today: [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub that never calls [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m, returns a hardcoded [38;5;153m{ success: true }[39m, and the app
  is a single [38;5;153mapp.js[39m with no module system, no build step, and one HTML page.

  One question at a time, starting with the one everything else hangs off.

  [1mThe fork:[0m a real account ID has to be [3missued[0m by something that knows the account exists. Right now nothing in this
  repo does — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m fabricates success without a network call. So either the authentication becomes real as part of
  this work, or the ID gets faked locally, and those two lead to very different designs.

  - [1mReal auth response is in scope[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m actually POSTs to [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m and the server returns the account ID. Buys
    a genuine identity that other forms can trust. Costs: real network code, error/failure paths, and a backend contract
    you'd have to pin down. This is the only option where "identify the actual user account" is literally true.
  - [1mStub keeps faking it, shaped like the real thing[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m stays offline but returns a synthesized ID through the
    same interface a real response would use. Buys the full client-side design (storage, accessor, other forms) now,
    with a one-function swap later. Costs: the ID is not real until the backend lands. Cheap to change.
  - [1mUsername [3mis[0;1m the identity[0m — no separate ID; persist the username and treat it as the account key. Buys the least
    machinery. Costs: breaks the day someone renames an account, and migrating off a username key later means touching
    every stored record. Expensive to reverse.

  [1mMy recommendation: the stubbed-but-real-shaped option.[0m It lets us design the persistence and cross-app access properly
  — which is the part you said matters — without inventing a backend contract in a repo whose login has never made a
  network call. The swap to real auth later touches one function.
